JS8CALL Map for Debian, Ubuntu and Raspberry Pi OS
==================================================

JS8CALL must be running with its TCP server enabled (File > Settings >
Reporting > Enable TCP Server, port 2237) and Max TCP Connections set to 3.

On a desktop
------------
Start "JS8CALL Map" from the applications menu (under Internet / Ham Radio),
or run `js8call-map` in a terminal. It opens the map in your web browser;
the terminal window shows its log, and closing it stops the map.

Headless (e.g. a Raspberry Pi without a monitor)
------------------------------------------------
Run it as a systemd user service, as the same user that runs JS8CALL:

    mkdir -p ~/.config/js8call-map
    echo '{ "webHost": "0.0.0.0" }' > ~/.config/js8call-map/config.json
    systemctl --user enable --now js8call-map
    sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER      # start at boot without logging in

Then browse to http://<the-pi's-address>:8181 from any device on your
network (`journalctl --user -u js8call-map` prints the address). If a
firewall is enabled:  sudo ufw allow 8181/tcp

Settings
--------
Settings are read from /etc/js8call-map/config.json, then
~/.config/js8call-map/config.json (which wins), then environment variables and
command-line options. Run `js8call-map --help` for the full list.

Upstream documentation: https://github.com/MRC757/JS8CALL-Map
